th District leans Republican by about seven points, and incumbent David Rouzer enters the general election with the advantages that usually decide open-seat-less races like this: an easy renomination, a large financial edge, and a steady stream of constituent-focused activity. Democratic challenger Kimberly Hardy has raised under $300,000 and reported just $65,000 in cash, leaving her little capacity to compete on the airwaves, and outside spending on either side has been negligible.\n\nThe available evidence converges. The only public poll, conducted in August by a Democratic-sponsored firm, showed Rouzer ahead 45 to 39. Prediction markets put his win probability above 80 percent, and every major race rater, from the Cook Political Report to Sabato's Crystal Ball, places the seat on the Republican side of the ledger, most calling it Likely or Solid Republican. The chief variable is the national political environment, which favors Democrats this cycle but would need to run considerably stronger than current indications to put the seat in play.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0898, "margin": -8.11}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0938, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0915, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.1198, "margin": -8.2}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0905, "margin": -8.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0678, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0876, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.0761, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.0866, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.1136, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.1231, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.075, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.1106, "margin": -8.5}]}